    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p64-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $5x^2+4x+1$ can be written as $(ax+b)^2+(cx+d)^2$ for some $a,b,c,d$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$5x^2+4x+1=(2x+1)^2+x^2$. One choice is $a=2,b=1,c=1,d=0$.",
          "worked": "Expanding and equating coefficients gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(ax+b)^2+(cx+d)^2&=(a^2+c^2)x^2+(2ab+2cd)x+b^2+d^2\\\\\na^2+c^2&=5\\\\\n2ab+2cd&=4\\\\\nb^2+d^2&=1.\n\\end{align*}$$\nChoose $b=1$ and $d=0$. Then $2a=4$, so $a=2$, and $c^2=1$. Choose $c=1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(2x+1)^2+x^2&=4x^2+4x+1+x^2\\\\\n&=5x^2+4x+1.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p64-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence explain why $5x^2+4x+1$ can never be negative for any real value of $x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "It is the sum of two squared terms, so $5x^2+4x+1\\geq0$ for all real $x$.",
          "worked": "For every real $x$, both $(2x+1)^2$ and $x^2$ are non-negative. Therefore\n$$\\begin{align*}\n5x^2+4x+1&=(2x+1)^2+x^2\\\\\n&\\geq0.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ]
  },
